\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \emph  {The corrected view and the vocabulary artifact} (cluster J). \textbf  {(A)} Fair non-linear ($k$NN) probe, six FM arms over five families (best per atlas). On the 11 \emph  {vocabulary-matched} human atlases (blue) the FM sits on/near the diagonal (this best-of view carries a $+0.024$ max-selection edge; unbiased parity is the per-\emph  {family} test of Table~\ref {tab:parity_combined}); the off-diagonal floor is the 8 cross-species mouse-symbol atlases (orange), where the tokenizer maps almost no genes ($k$NN${\approx }$0.53)---a tokenization handicap; the 9th (ENSG-only) sits near parity because its FM still maps ${\sim }24\%$ of genes. \textbf  {(B)} Reference-free $R^2_{\mathrm  {expr}}$: points above the diagonal mean the cell-type labels explain \emph  {less} HVG-expression variance than the PCA clustering (20/20 atlases; both clusterings 11/20)---the labels are a \emph  {coarser} partition than clustering recovers, corroborating the circularity. Together (A)--(B): with vocabulary mismatch and label circularity separated out, zero-shot scFMs \emph  {neither beat nor trail} PCA on coarse human cell types. \textbf  {(C)} Vocabulary dose--response: $x$ = fraction of atlas genes in the FM vocabulary, $y$ = $k$NN-AUROC. Quality tracks coverage ($\rho =0.83$, $r=0.93$) but as a \emph  {threshold}---mismatch at $x\!\approx \!0$, matched at $[0.32,0.94]$, little between; the within-matched gradient is weak ($\rho \approx 0.34$--$0.40$). \textbf  {(D)} Causal within-atlas ablation (three fixed atlases, only a fraction of readable genes kept, re-embedded; $x$ log scale): AUROC flat at parity (grey $=$ full-gene PCA) to ${\sim }1$--$2$k genes, then cliffs to 0.50 at zero---the cross-species ``FM failures'' are a tokenization cliff, not a representational deficit.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fair}{{3}{7}{\emph {The corrected view and the vocabulary artifact} (cluster J). \textbf {(A)} Fair non-linear ($k$NN) probe, six FM arms over five families (best per atlas). On the 11 \emph {vocabulary-matched} human atlases (blue) the FM sits on/near the diagonal (this best-of view carries a $+0.024$ max-selection edge; unbiased parity is the per-\emph {family} test of Table~\ref {tab:parity_combined}); the off-diagonal floor is the 8 cross-species mouse-symbol atlases (orange), where the tokenizer maps almost no genes ($k$NN${\approx }$0.53)---a tokenization handicap; the 9th (ENSG-only) sits near parity because its FM still maps ${\sim }24\%$ of genes. \textbf {(B)} Reference-free $R^2_{\mathrm {expr}}$: points above the diagonal mean the cell-type labels explain \emph {less} HVG-expression variance than the PCA clustering (20/20 atlases; both clusterings 11/20)---the labels are a \emph {coarser} partition than clustering recovers, corroborating the circularity. Together (A)--(B): with vocabulary mismatch and label circularity separated out, zero-shot scFMs \emph {neither beat nor trail} PCA on coarse human cell types. \textbf {(C)} Vocabulary dose--response: $x$ = fraction of atlas genes in the FM vocabulary, $y$ = $k$NN-AUROC. Quality tracks coverage ($\rho =0.83$, $r=0.93$) but as a \emph {threshold}---mismatch at $x\!\approx \!0$, matched at $[0.32,0.94]$, little between; the within-matched gradient is weak ($\rho \approx 0.34$--$0.40$). \textbf {(D)} Causal within-atlas ablation (three fixed atlases, only a fraction of readable genes kept, re-embedded; $x$ log scale): AUROC flat at parity (grey $=$ full-gene PCA) to ${\sim }1$--$2$k genes, then cliffs to 0.50 at zero---the cross-species ``FM failures'' are a tokenization cliff, not a representational deficit}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces scATAC combined comparison and reliability profile (cluster I). \textbf  {Top} (matched 20{,}002-cell comparison): neither of two independent scATAC FMs beats the simple full-peak LSI baseline; calibration (ECE) tracks \emph  {dimensionality}, not FM-vs-baseline (192-d Atacformer as well-calibrated as the 50-d SVD, 2048-d ChromFound not); conformal coverage robust for all. \textbf  {Bottom} (full 130{,}418-cell profile; peak-LSI is the full cohort, the two FMs the matched subset): peak-LSI and \emph  {both} FMs stay well-calibrated and keep coverage under cross-sample and Control$\to $AD shift, with a depth-deconfounded confidence signal and negative controls at/below chance---the positive counterpart to the scRNA collapse (cluster K); rare-type (pericyte/endothelial) over-rejection is the shared fairness cost. Top/bottom use separate splits, so shared-baseline estimates differ slightly.}}{7}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:scatac_combined}{{II}{7}{scATAC combined comparison and reliability profile (cluster I). \textbf {Top} (matched 20{,}002-cell comparison): neither of two independent scATAC FMs beats the simple full-peak LSI baseline; calibration (ECE) tracks \emph {dimensionality}, not FM-vs-baseline (192-d Atacformer as well-calibrated as the 50-d SVD, 2048-d ChromFound not); conformal coverage robust for all. \textbf {Bottom} (full 130{,}418-cell profile; peak-LSI is the full cohort, the two FMs the matched subset): peak-LSI and \emph {both} FMs stay well-calibrated and keep coverage under cross-sample and Control$\to $AD shift, with a depth-deconfounded confidence signal and negative controls at/below chance---the positive counterpart to the scRNA collapse (cluster K); rare-type (pericyte/endothelial) over-rejection is the shared fairness cost. Top/bottom use separate splits, so shared-baseline estimates differ slightly}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {IV-F}}Self-computed scRNA suite: representation (cluster J)}{7}{subsection.4.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Cluster-H fair re-check and spatial dose--response (niche-ID). \textbf  {(A)} unsupervised ARI-vs-label (orange) says the spatial FMs fail, but a supervised $k$NN niche-probe (blue) shows all three carry niche information (AUROC 0.76--0.86); the residual gap to spatial baselines is small. \textbf  {(B)} the niche labels are composition-defined ($R^2_{\mathrm  {compo}}$ 0.234, near-zero single-cell $R^2_{\mathrm  {expr}}$ 0.007), so the composition baseline winning ARI is near-tautological---the same circularity as cluster J. \textbf  {(C--D)} spatial dose--response: per-cell representations are progressively smoothed over $k$ spatial neighbours ($x$ log scale, $k{=}0$ per-cell), scored by supervised niche prediction (spatially-blocked CV): \textbf  {(C)} $k$NN-AUROC, \textbf  {(D)} macro-F1. Smoothing per-cell PCA (blue) lifts prediction monotonically (F1 $\rho =0.98$) past all three raw FMs and the composition baseline by $k\approx 25$--$50$; the same dose helps a smoothed FM (orange). The residual ``spatial-FM gap'' is thus the spatial-aggregation axis---one operation a per-cell model lacks---not a representational deficit.}}{8}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spfair}{{4}{8}{Cluster-H fair re-check and spatial dose--response (niche-ID). \textbf {(A)} unsupervised ARI-vs-label (orange) says the spatial FMs fail, but a supervised $k$NN niche-probe (blue) shows all three carry niche information (AUROC 0.76--0.86); the residual gap to spatial baselines is small. \textbf {(B)} the niche labels are composition-defined ($R^2_{\mathrm {compo}}$ 0.234, near-zero single-cell $R^2_{\mathrm {expr}}$ 0.007), so the composition baseline winning ARI is near-tautological---the same circularity as cluster J. \textbf {(C--D)} spatial dose--response: per-cell representations are progressively smoothed over $k$ spatial neighbours ($x$ log scale, $k{=}0$ per-cell), scored by supervised niche prediction (spatially-blocked CV): \textbf {(C)} $k$NN-AUROC, \textbf {(D)} macro-F1. Smoothing per-cell PCA (blue) lifts prediction monotonically (F1 $\rho =0.98$) past all three raw FMs and the composition baseline by $k\approx 25$--$50$; the same dose helps a smoothed FM (orange). The residual ``spatial-FM gap'' is thus the spatial-aggregation axis---one operation a per-cell model lacks---not a representational deficit}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \emph  {scATAC (cluster I).} \textbf  {(A)} Conformal coverage holds under cross-sample and cross-disease shift, whereas the scRNA scGPT regime collapses. \textbf  {(B,C)} Matched comparison of two scATAC FMs against simple LSI baselines: neither FM beats full-peak LSI on accuracy, and the ECE difference is a representation-dimensionality effect.}}{8}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scatac}{{5}{8}{\emph {scATAC (cluster I).} \textbf {(A)} Conformal coverage holds under cross-sample and cross-disease shift, whereas the scRNA scGPT regime collapses. \textbf {(B,C)} Matched comparison of two scATAC FMs against simple LSI baselines: neither FM beats full-peak LSI on accuracy, and the ECE difference is a representation-dimensionality effect}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \emph  {Batch-shift dose--response and modality contrast, on one ruler.} $x$ = batch-shift strength (cross-validated AUROC for held-out-batch/-sample membership; 0.5 exchangeable, 1.0 separable); $y$ = cross-batch conformal coverage gap; axes shared. \textbf  {(A)} scRNA: blue $=$ the 24-atlas $\times $ 4-classical-method sweep (shift 0.13--1.0; fitted curve and $\rho =0.82$ are PCA+logreg); three FM families (orange, vocabulary-matched atlases) reach only the high-shift end ($\ge 0.96$) and collapse by the same amount as classical ($+0.48$ vs.\ $+0.49$)---FMs do not repair it. \textbf  {(B)} scATAC (same axes): peak-LSI plus two FMs (ChromFound, Atacformer) give a \emph  {flat} curve---gap near zero throughout ($\rho =-0.24$, $n.s.$; mean $-0.005$, max $+0.08$). The scRNA collapse also holds for the ECE gap ($\rho =0.76$) and a classifier-free centroid-distance measure ($\rho =0.58$--$0.60$). Coverage collapse is a graded property of the scRNA cross-batch regime---shared by every method there, absent in scATAC.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:batchdose}{{6}{8}{\emph {Batch-shift dose--response and modality contrast, on one ruler.} $x$ = batch-shift strength (cross-validated AUROC for held-out-batch/-sample membership; 0.5 exchangeable, 1.0 separable); $y$ = cross-batch conformal coverage gap; axes shared. \textbf {(A)} scRNA: blue $=$ the 24-atlas $\times $ 4-classical-method sweep (shift 0.13--1.0; fitted curve and $\rho =0.82$ are PCA+logreg); three FM families (orange, vocabulary-matched atlases) reach only the high-shift end ($\ge 0.96$) and collapse by the same amount as classical ($+0.48$ vs.\ $+0.49$)---FMs do not repair it. \textbf {(B)} scATAC (same axes): peak-LSI plus two FMs (ChromFound, Atacformer) give a \emph {flat} curve---gap near zero throughout ($\rho =-0.24$, $n.s.$; mean $-0.005$, max $+0.08$). The scRNA collapse also holds for the ECE gap ($\rho =0.76$) and a classifier-free centroid-distance measure ($\rho =0.58$--$0.60$). Coverage collapse is a graded property of the scRNA cross-batch regime---shared by every method there, absent in scATAC}{figure.6}{}}
